WEBVTT

00:00:00.345 --> 00:00:03.220
Writing detection rules is not merely a technical exercise.

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:06.800
It requires curiosity, communication, operational awareness,

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:10.770
and empathy for analysts who rely on alerts during stressful situations.

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:14.205
Effective detection engineers focus on outcomes rather than alert counts.

00:00:14.205 --> 00:00:16.940
They ask whether a rule helps defenders make better decisions,

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:18.770
whether it fits the investigation workflow,

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:21.695
and whether it gives enough context to act responsibly.

00:00:21.695 --> 00:00:23.160
Collaboration matters.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:26.460
Detection engineers benefit from feedback from SOC analysts, incident responders,

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:31.960
threat hunters, IT teams, cloud teams, identity teams, and application owners.

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:35.895
Each group understands part of the environment and can help improve signal quality.

00:00:35.895 --> 00:00:39.170
Sustainable detection engineering depends on habits: document decisions,

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:41.950
track assumptions, test changes, review performance,

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:46.400
retire rules that no longer add value, and improve detections as the environment changes.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.370
The objective is helping defenders identify and investigate activity that matters.
